sec.p:
HiLo microscopy (Lim et al., 2008; Mertz and Kim, 2010; Mertz, 2011; Ford et al., 2012) is one of the implementations of optical sectioning structured illumination microscopy (OS-SIM) (Neil et al., 1997; Keller et al., 2010; Chen et al., 2023; Breuninger et al., 2007; Kalchmair et al., 2010), which uses patterned illumination to modulate in-focus signals and produce an optically sectioned image with out-of-focus background suppressed. In HiLo microscopy, a uniformly illuminated image (U(ρ→)\begin{document}$(U(\vec{\rho})$\end{document}, where ρ→\begin{document}$\vec{\rho}$\end{document} represents the 2D spatial coordinate) and a structured-illumination image (S(ρ→))\begin{document}$(S(\vec{\rho}))$\end{document} are required to recover the high-frequency and low-frequency components of the in-focus signal. They can be written respectively as (Mertz and Kim, 2010):
(1)
U(ρ→)=Iin(ρ→)+Iout(ρ→) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} U(\vec{\rho})=I_{\text {in }}(\vec{\rho})+I_{\text {out }}(\vec{\rho}) \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(2)
S(ρ→)=12[Iin(ρ→)(1+Msin⁡(kgx))+Iout(ρ→)] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} S(\vec{\rho})=\frac{1}{2}\left[I_{\text {in }}(\vec{\rho})\left(1+M \sin \left(k_{g} x\right)\right)+I_{\text {out }}(\vec{\rho})\right] \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where Iin(ρ→)\begin{document}$I_{\text {in }}(\vec{\rho})$\end{document} and Iout(ρ→)\begin{document}$I_{\text {out }}(\vec{\rho})$\end{document} are in-focus and out-of-focus signals, respectively. In S(ρ→)\begin{document}$S(\vec{\rho})$\end{document}, the in-focus illumination is modulated in a sinusoid pattern in the x-direction with frequency kg and contrast M.

Because the high-frequency components attenuate quickly during defocusing, Iout(ρ→)\begin{document}$I_{\text {out }}(\vec{\rho})$\end{document} contains mainly low-frequency information, which means HP[Iout(ρ→)]≈0\begin{document}$H P\left[I_{\text {out }}(\vec{\rho})\right] \approx 0$\end{document} Thus, the high-frequency components of Iin(ρ→)\begin{document}$I_{\text {in }}(\vec{\rho})$\end{document} can be directly recovered by using a high-pass filter (the cutoff frequency was much smaller than kg) to U(ρ→)\begin{document}$U(\vec{\rho})$\end{document}, obtaining
(3)
HP[Iin(ρ→)]≈HP[U(ρ→) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} H P\left[I_{\text {in }}(\vec{\rho})\right] \approx H P[U(\vec{\rho}) \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

To recover the lost low-frequency components of the in-focus signal, a demodulated image is obtained firstly:
(4)
D(ρ→)=|HP[U(ρ→)−2S(ρ→)]|=Iin(ρ→)M∣sin⁡(kgx) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} D(\vec{\rho})=|H P[U(\vec{\rho})-2 S(\vec{\rho})]|=I_{\text {in }}(\vec{\rho}) M \mid \sin \left(k_{g} x\right) \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

By applying a low-pass filter directly to this demodulated image, we then get
(5)
LP[Iin(ρ→)]≈π2MLP[D(ρ→)] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} L P\left[I_{\text {in }}(\vec{\rho})\right] \approx \frac{\pi}{2 M} L P[D(\vec{\rho})] \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
